#
# Ask:
#
#
#       How many Kubernetes clusters exist?
#
#       Which clouds host them?
#
#       Which services span several clusters?
#
#       Which services span several regions?
#
#       Which services expose several network entry points?
#
#       Which endpoint observations actually matter to routing?
#
#       Which observations proved useful during incidents?
#
#       Which infrastructure facts turned out to be noise?
#
#
# Then decide whether the endpoint abstraction needs to change.
#
#
#       OPERATIONAL EVIDENCE
#           ->
#       DOMAIN REQUIREMENT
#           ->
#       CONTRACT
#           ->
#       BEHAVIOR
#
#
# not:
#
#
#       KUBERNETES HAS A FIELD
#           ->
#       AGENT 11 GETS A FIELD
#
#
# ============================================================================
# THE FUTURE PROBLEM
# ============================================================================
#
# SEIR-I can conceptually tolerate:
#
#
#       AIService.service_id
#               |
#               v
#       endpoint evidence
#
#
# Future infrastructure may instead look like:
#
#
#       AIModel
#           |
#           v
#       AIService
#           |
#       +---+------------------------------------------------------+
#       |                                                          |
#       v                                                          v
#   Deployment A                                              Deployment B
#       |                                                          |
#       +-------------------+                                      |
#       |                   |                                      |
#       v                   v                                      v
#   Cluster A           Cluster B                              Cluster C
#       |                   |                                      |
#       +----+----+          +----+----+                            |
#       |         |          |         |                            |
#       v         v          v         v                            v
#     End A1    End A2     End B1    End B2                       End C1
#
#
# At that point:
#
#
#       service_id
#
#
# may no longer be sufficiently precise network identity.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# CURRENT CORRELATION KEY != PERMANENT DOMAIN IDENTITY
# ============================================================================
#
# SEIR-I currently correlates endpoint evidence using:
#
#
#       service_id
#
#
# That is a convenience based upon the domain information currently
# available.
#
#
# It must not become a permanent assumption merely because it worked first.
#
#
# Future evidence may need to become scoped by:
#
#
#       deployment
#
#       cluster
#
#       endpoint
#
#
# or another architecture not yet known.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — DO WE NEED deployment_id?
# ============================================================================
#
# If one logical AI service is deployed more than once:
#
#
#       AIService
#           |
#           +--> Deployment A
#           |
#           +--> Deployment B
#
#
# then service-level endpoint evidence may be too coarse.
#
#
# A future deployment contract might eventually distinguish:
#
#
#       deployment identity
#
#       provider
#
#       region
#
#       runtime environment
#
#       operational lifecycle
#
#
# But:
#
#
#       POSSIBLE deployment_id
#           !=
#       REQUIRED CURRENT FIELD
#
#
# Do not create the model until routing/network behavior actually needs this
# distinction.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — DO WE NEED endpoint_id?
# ============================================================================
#
# Likewise, one deployment may expose more than one endpoint.
#
#
# Example:
#
#
#       Deployment A
#           |
#           +--> private endpoint
#           |
#           +--> public endpoint
#           |
#           +--> internal load balancer
#
#
# Future architecture may need first-class endpoint identity.
#
#
# But do not assume:
#
#
#       ENDPOINT
#           =
#       ONE IP ADDRESS
#
#
# Modern infrastructure makes endpoint identity more complicated.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# WHAT IS AN ENDPOINT?
# ============================================================================
#
# Future systems may expose several different concepts that humans casually
# call an endpoint:
#
#
#       Kubernetes Service
#
#       ClusterIP
#
#       LoadBalancer address
#
#       Ingress host
#
#       Gateway listener
#
#       HTTPRoute
#
#       GRPCRoute
#
#       service-mesh virtual service
#
#       private cloud endpoint
#
#       resolved backend address
#
#       DNS name
#
#
# These are not automatically the same concept.
#
#
# Before creating NetworkEndpoint, determine which identity Agent 11 actually
# needs.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# ENDPOINT MAY BE LOGICAL OR PHYSICAL
# ============================================================================
#
# A future endpoint could represent:
#
#
#       logical destination
#
#
# or:
#
#
#       resolved network destination
#
#
# Those are different.
#
#
# Example:
#
#
#       llm-service.prod.svc.cluster.local
#
#
# may resolve through Kubernetes service networking to several changing
# backend addresses.
#
#
# Therefore:
#
#
#       LOGICAL ENDPOINT
#           !=
#       RESOLVED BACKEND
#
#
# ============================================================================
# INFRASTRUCTURE COMPLEXITY SHOULD BE TRANSLATED, NOT REPLICATED
# ============================================================================
#
# Kubernetes may expose:
#
#
#       Service
#
#       EndpointSlice
#
#       Pod
#
#       Deployment
#
#       StatefulSet
#
#       Gateway
#
#       HTTPRoute
#
#       GRPCRoute
#
#       NetworkPolicy
#
#
# Agent 11 does not need matching domain objects for every one of them.
#
#
# The adapter should translate:
#
#
#       INFRASTRUCTURE COMPLEXITY
#
#
# into:
#
#
#       THE SMALLEST USEFUL AGENT 11 EVIDENCE
#
#
# ============================================================================
# MULTI-CLOUD KUBERNETES
# ============================================================================
#
# Future Agent 11 may encounter:
#
#
#       AWS EKS
#
#       Azure AKS
#
#       Google GKE
#
#       Oracle OKE
#
#       on-premises Kubernetes
#
#
# potentially all within the same organization.
#
#
# Preserve the distinctions:
#
#
#       ROUTING DOMAIN
#           !=
#       CLOUD PROVIDER
#           !=
#       ORCHESTRATION PLATFORM
#           !=
#       CLUSTER
#
#
