#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — CLUSTER IDENTITY
# ============================================================================
#
# Multi-cluster endpoint discovery eventually requires knowing:
#
#
#       WHICH CLUSTER?
#
#
# A Kubernetes Service named:
#
#
#       inference
#
#
# could exist in:
#
#
#       cluster A
#
#       cluster B
#
#       cluster C
#
#
# possibly with the same namespace.
#
#
# Therefore:
#
#
#       namespace + service name
#
#
# may not be globally unique across the organization.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# CLUSTER IDENTITY != CLOUD PROVIDER IDENTITY
# ============================================================================
#
# One cloud may contain many clusters.
#
#
# One organization may run several clusters in one region.
#
#
# Therefore:
#
#
#       AWS
#
#
# is not enough to identify:
#
#
#       which Kubernetes control plane
#
#
# produced endpoint evidence.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# ONE ADAPTER INSTANCE MAY REPRESENT ONE CLUSTER
# ============================================================================
#
# One future architecture may instantiate:
#
#
#       KubernetesEndpointEvidenceSource(cluster_a_client)
#
#       KubernetesEndpointEvidenceSource(cluster_b_client)
#
#       KubernetesEndpointEvidenceSource(cluster_c_client)
#
#
# Each adapter instance could represent one Kubernetes observation boundary.
#
#
# Then a higher component might aggregate endpoint evidence.
#
#
# This may be cleaner than one enormous adapter responsible for every cluster
# in the organization.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# MULTI-CLUSTER AGGREGATION MAY BELONG ABOVE THE ADAPTER
# ============================================================================
#
# Conceptually:
#
#
#       Cluster A Client
#            |
#            v
#       Endpoint Source A
#            |
#            +---------------------+
#                                  |
#       Cluster B Client           |
#            |                     |
#            v                     |
#       Endpoint Source B ---------+--> Network aggregation
#                                  |
#       Cluster C Client           |
#            |                     |
#            v                     |
#       Endpoint Source C ---------+
#
#
# Exact ownership remains intentionally open.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# GLOBAL KUBERNETES CLIENT REMAINS A BAD IDEA
# ============================================================================
#
# Avoid:
#
#
#       global_kubernetes_api
#
#
# because it silently encodes:
#
#
#       one cluster
#
#       one identity
#
#       one credential lifecycle
#
#       one availability domain
#
#
# IoC should keep these dependencies explicit.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# DEPENDENCY LIFETIME != DOMAIN SEMANTICS
# ============================================================================
#
# Future Kubernetes clients may have lifecycle concerns such as:
#
#
#       credential refresh
#
#       watch reconnect
#
#       connection pooling
#
#       cluster rotation
#
#       token expiration
#
#
# Those are dependency/runtime concerns.
#
#
# They do not redefine:
#
#
#       what endpoint evidence means.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# IoC CONTAINER MAY EVENTUALLY APPEAR
# ============================================================================
#
# Manual constructor injection is sufficient for SEIR-I.
#
#
# Future application composition may eventually become complex enough to use
# a DI/container mechanism.
#
#
# If so:
#
#
#       CONTAINER
#
#
# should manage:
#
#
#       construction
#
#       dependency lifecycle
#
#       implementation selection
#
#
# It should not define:
#
#
#       Agent 11 endpoint semantics
#
#
# ============================================================================
# DI TOOLING != DOMAIN DESIGN
# ============================================================================
#
# Never allow:
#
#
#       framework annotation
#
#
# to become the reason a domain dependency exists.
#
#
# Dependency direction should still follow architecture.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — GATEWAY API
# ============================================================================
#
# Kubernetes Gateway API may increasingly become a common ingress/routing
# mechanism for AI services.
#
#
# Future infrastructure may look like:
#
#
#       Agent 11
#           |
#           v
#       Gateway
#           |
#           v
#       HTTPRoute / GRPCRoute
#           |
#           v
#       Kubernetes Service
#           |
#           v
#       EndpointSlice
#           |
#           v
#       backend workload
#
#
# Agent 11 should not reproduce this object graph internally unless its
# domain actually needs those distinctions.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# Gateway != Agent 11 Router
# ============================================================================
#
# Kubernetes Gateway routing answers:
#
#
#       HOW NETWORK TRAFFIC IS FORWARDED
#
#
# Agent 11 routing answers:
#
#
#       WHICH AI DESTINATION SHOULD RECEIVE THE REQUEST
#
#
# Therefore:
#
#
#       Gateway route
#           !=
#       RoutingDecision
#
#
# ============================================================================
# HTTPRoute != AI DATA POLICY
# ============================================================================
#
# A Kubernetes HTTPRoute may permit traffic to a backend.
#
#
# That does not establish:
#
#
#       E7/E8/E9 authorization
#
#
# ============================================================================
# GRPCRoute != AI DATA POLICY
# ============================================================================
#
# Same rule.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — SERVICE MESH
# ============================================================================
#
# Future clusters may use:
#
#
#       Istio
#
#       Linkerd
#
#       another service mesh
#
#
# Mesh infrastructure may provide:
#
#
#       service discovery
#
#       mTLS
#
#       retries
#
#       traffic splitting
#
#       telemetry
#
#       outlier detection
#
#       local load balancing
#
#
# Agent 11 should not duplicate those responsibilities without a specific
# reason.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# MESH CONNECTIVITY != AI AUTHORIZATION
# ============================================================================
#
# If a service mesh reports:
#
#
#       workload A can communicate with workload B
#
#
# that means:
#
#
#       INFRASTRUCTURE COMMUNICATION IS PERMITTED
#
#
# It does not mean:
#
#
#       AGENT 11 DATA POLICY PERMITS THIS AI REQUEST
#
#
# ============================================================================
# MESH mTLS != AI DATA APPROVAL
# ============================================================================
#
# mTLS may establish:
#
#
#       transport encryption
#
#       workload identity
#
#
# It does not establish:
#
#
#       data classification authorization
#
#
# ============================================================================
# VIRTUALSERVICE ROUTE != RoutingDecision
# ============================================================================
#
# A mesh may route traffic based upon:
#
#
#       host
#
#       headers
#
#       weights
#
#       topology
#
#
# That remains infrastructure behavior.
#
#
# Agent 11's routing decision remains a higher-level AI selection decision.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# RETRY MULTIPLICATION
# ============================================================================
#
# Future-you: watch this carefully.
#
#
# Several infrastructure layers may independently retry:
#
#
#       Agent 11
#
#       provider SDK
#
#       HTTP client
#
#       service mesh
#
#       gateway
#
#
# Example:
#
#
#       Agent 11 retry = 3
#
#       provider SDK retry = 3
#
#       service mesh retry = 3
#
#
# may create far more attempts than the engineer expects.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# APPLICATION RETRY x INFRASTRUCTURE RETRY = SURPRISE
# ============================================================================
#
# Preserve:
#
#
#       AGENT 11 RETRY
#           !=
#       SERVICE MESH RETRY
#
#
#       SERVICE MESH RETRY
#           !=
#       PROVIDER SDK RETRY
#
#
# Future recovery architecture must understand retry ownership.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# RETRY MULTIPLICATION MAY MULTIPLY COST
# ============================================================================
#
# For AI inference, retries may multiply:
#
#
#       token usage
#
#       latency
#
#       provider charges
#
#       duplicate computation
#
#
# Therefore retry behavior cannot be ignored as an infrastructure detail.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# RETRY MULTIPLICATION MAY MULTIPLY DATA DISCLOSURE
# ============================================================================
#
# If retries cross:
#
#
#       endpoints
#
#       providers
#
#       regions
#
#
# they may also affect:
#
#
#       data residency
#
#       disclosure
#
#       audit
#
#
# Recovery design must remain policy-aware at the Agent 11 layer.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — PRIVATE ENDPOINTS
# ============================================================================
#
# Cloud environments may expose AI services using:
#
#
#       AWS PrivateLink
#
#       Azure Private Endpoint
#
#       GCP Private Service Connect
#
#       OCI private networking
#
#       Kubernetes internal load balancers
#
#
# These describe network exposure/connectivity characteristics.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# PRIVATE != AUTHORIZED
# ============================================================================
#
# A private network path may reduce exposure.
#
#
# It does not automatically authorize:
#
#
#       E7
#
#       E8
#
#       E9
#
#
# data.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# PUBLIC != AUTOMATICALLY PROHIBITED
# ============================================================================
#
# The inverse is also important.
#
#
# A public endpoint is not automatically forbidden unless policy says so.
#
#
# Therefore:
#
#
#       NETWORK EXPOSURE CHARACTERISTIC
#           !=
#       AI POLICY DECISION
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — ENDPOINT TYPE
# ============================================================================
#
# Future evidence may need to distinguish:
#
#
#       cluster-local
#
#       internal
#
#       private
#
#       public
#
#
# But only add such a field if:
#
#
#       routing
#
#       network evaluation
#
#       policy
#
#
# actually consumes it.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# ENDPOINT ATTRIBUTE != AUTHORIZATION
# ============================================================================
#
# Even if future NetworkEndpoint contains:
#
#
#       exposure_type = PRIVATE
#
#
# policy still independently determines:
#
#
#       whether the request may use it.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — CONTROL PLANE VS DATA PLANE
# ============================================================================
#
# Kubernetes endpoint discovery usually depends upon the Kubernetes API.
#
#
# But:
#
#
#       KUBERNETES API
#           =
#       CONTROL PLANE
#
#
# while:
#
#
#       inference traffic
#           =
#       DATA PLANE
#
#
# Those can fail independently.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# CONTROL PLANE DOWN != DATA PLANE DOWN
# ============================================================================
#
# Example:
#
#
#       Kubernetes API unavailable
#
#       existing Service networking still operational
#
#       existing Pods still serving requests
#
#
# Therefore:
#
#
#       DISCOVERY UNAVAILABLE
#           !=
#       INFERENCE UNAVAILABLE
#
#
# ============================================================================
# DATA PLANE DOWN != CONTROL PLANE DOWN
# ============================================================================
#
# The reverse may also occur.
#
#
# Kubernetes API may be healthy while:
#
#
#       route between Agent 11 and cluster is broken
#
#       firewall blocks traffic
#
#       VPN is down
#
#
# Therefore:
#
#
#       CONTROL PLANE HEALTH
#           !=
#       CALLER REACHABILITY
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — CONTRADICTORY EVIDENCE
# ============================================================================
#
# Consider:
#
#
#       EndpointSlice:
#           ready = true
#
#
#       service mesh:
#           backend healthy
#
#
#       network path evaluation:
#           unreachable from Agent 11
#
#
# All three observations may be simultaneously correct.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# CONTRADICTION MAY BE APPARENT, NOT REAL
# ============================================================================
#
# In the previous example:
#
#
#       endpoint exists
#
#       backend may be healthy
#
#       caller cannot reach it
#
#
# Therefore:
#
#
#       ENDPOINT EVIDENCE
#           !=
#       SERVICE HEALTH
#           !=
#       NETWORK PATH
#
#
# ============================================================================
# DO NOT COLLAPSE EVIDENCE INTO healthy=True
# ============================================================================
#
# Avoid future objects such as:
#
#
#       healthy = True
#
#
# attempting to represent:
#
#
#       endpoint existence
#
#       application health
#
#       network reachability
#
#       authorization
#
#
# simultaneously.
#
#
# Those are independent dimensions.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — WHO RESOLVES EVIDENCE?
# ============================================================================
#
# Eventually Agent 11 may consume evidence from:
#
#
#       Kubernetes
#
#       service mesh
#
#       cloud provider
#
#       network probes
#
#       health systems
#
#       service registry
#
#
# A future network-level component may need to combine or interpret them.
#
#
# That responsibility should probably NOT live in endpoint.py.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# ENDPOINT.PY SHOULD NOT BECOME NETWORK EVERYTHING
# ============================================================================
#
# Resist adding:
#
#
#       DNS resolution
#
#       TLS validation
#
#       service health
#
#       packet loss
#
#       latency
#
#       BGP
#
#       SD-WAN
#
#       VPN
#
#       NetworkPolicy interpretation
#
#       AI policy
#
#
# merely because these affect eventual connectivity.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# CURRENT PACKAGE RESPONSIBILITIES
# ============================================================================
#
# Keep conceptual separation:
#
#
#       endpoint.py
#
#           What endpoint evidence exists?
#
#
#       health.py
#
#           What operational-health evidence exists?
#
#
#       path.py
#
#           What network-path evidence exists?
#
#
# Future orchestration may combine them.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — EVIDENCE FRESHNESS
# ============================================================================
#
# Endpoint information changes over time.
#
#
# Example:
#
#
#       12:00:00
#           Pod A ready
#
#
#       12:00:04
#           Pod A gone
#
#
# A cached observation may still say:
#
#
#       ready
#
#
# after the infrastructure changed.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# OBSERVED THEN != TRUE NOW
# ============================================================================
#
# Preserve:
#
#
#       ENDPOINT WAS PRESENT
#           !=
#       ENDPOINT IS PRESENT
#
#
#       ENDPOINT WAS READY
#           !=
#       ENDPOINT IS READY
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE EVIDENCE MAY NEED observed_at
# ============================================================================
#
# If endpoint evidence becomes cached or distributed, a future contract may
# eventually require:
#
#
#       observed_at
#
#
# or equivalent temporal context.
#
#
# But:
#
#
#       POSSIBLE FUTURE TIMESTAMP
#           !=
#       ADD IT NOW
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE EVIDENCE MAY NEED source
# ============================================================================
#
# If several systems report endpoint state, Future Agent 11 may need to know:
#
#
#       source = kubernetes
#
#       source = service mesh
#
#       source = registry
#
#
# But do not add a generic source field without knowing how provenance will
# actually be used.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE EVIDENCE MAY NEED freshness semantics
# ============================================================================
#
# Different evidence sources may age differently.
#
#
# A Kubernetes watch may be near-real-time.
#
# A registry snapshot may refresh every minute.
#
# A cloud inventory may refresh every several minutes.
#
#
# Therefore:
#
#
#       FRESH
#
#
# may not be one universal Boolean.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# STALE != FALSE
# ============================================================================
#
# If evidence is stale:
#
#
#       STALE
#
#
# does not necessarily mean:
#
#
#       endpoint absent
#
#
# It means:
#
#
#       current truth is not sufficiently established.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FAIL CLOSED != FALSIFY STATE
# ============================================================================
#
# Agent 11 may conservatively refuse to treat stale evidence as sufficient
# for routing.
#
#
# But it should preserve:
#
#
#       STALE
#
#       UNKNOWN
#
#       OBSERVATION FAILED
#
#
# rather than rewriting everything as:
#
#
#       ABSENT
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — ASYNC
# ============================================================================
#
# Kubernetes watches, service-mesh telemetry, and cloud APIs may encourage
# asynchronous infrastructure code.
#
#
# That does not automatically mean:
#
#
#       EndpointEvidenceSource.has_endpoint(...)
#
#
# must become async.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# ASYNC OBSERVATION + SYNC EVALUATION MAY BE VALID
# ============================================================================
#
# A future design may use:
#
#
#       async Kubernetes watch
#               |
#               v
#       local evidence cache
#               |
#               v
#       synchronous endpoint evaluation
#
#
# This can preserve simple domain behavior while infrastructure updates occur
# asynchronously.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# DO NOT MAKE DOMAIN ASYNC JUST BECAUSE INFRASTRUCTURE IS ASYNC
# ============================================================================
#
# Async is an execution model.
#
#
# It is not a domain semantic.
#
#
#       ASYNC
#           !=
#       BETTER ARCHITECTURE
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — WATCH RECOVERY
# ============================================================================
#
# Kubernetes watches may:
#
#
#       disconnect
#
#       time out
#
#       require resource-version restart
#
#       experience API failover
#
#
# Those are adapter/runtime concerns.
#
#
# NetworkEndpointEvaluator should not handle them.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# WATCH HEALTH != ENDPOINT HEALTH
# ============================================================================
#
# If the watch connection fails:
#
#
#       OBSERVATION CHANNEL FAILED
#
#
# not necessarily:
#
#
#       ENDPOINT FAILED
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — MULTIPLE ENDPOINTS
# ============================================================================
#
# A service may have many endpoint records.
#
#
# Future routing/network logic may eventually care about:
#
#
#       one usable endpoint exists
#
#       several usable endpoints exist
#
#       endpoint diversity
#
#       deployment diversity
#
#
# But endpoint count itself does not establish resilience.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# THREE ENDPOINTS != THREE FAILURE DOMAINS
# ============================================================================
#
# Example:
#
#
#       Endpoint A
#
#       Endpoint B
#
#       Endpoint C
#
#
# all terminate on:
#
#
#       same load balancer
#
#       same cluster
#
#       same region
#
#
# Then:
#
#
#       THREE ENDPOINTS
#
#
# may still represent:
#
#
#       ONE LARGE FAILURE DOMAIN
#
#
# ============================================================================
# ENDPOINT DIVERSITY != FAILURE-DOMAIN DIVERSITY
# ============================================================================
#
# Preserve:
#
#
#       DIFFERENT ADDRESS
#           !=
#       INDEPENDENT FAILURE
#
#
# ============================================================================
# SAME SERVICE MAY HAVE REAL FAILURE-DOMAIN DIVERSITY
# ============================================================================
#
# Conversely:
#
#
#       same AIService
#
#
# may eventually exist in:
#
#
#       GKE Tokyo
#
#       AKS Virginia
#
#       on-premises Austin
#
#
# Those may offer meaningful infrastructure diversity.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# MODEL DIVERSITY != FAILURE-DOMAIN DIVERSITY
# ============================================================================
#
# Different FMs might still be exposed through:
#
#
#       same cluster
#
#       same provider
#
#       same region
#
#       same gateway
#
#
# Therefore:
#
#
#       DIFFERENT MODEL
#           !=
#       DIFFERENT FAILURE DOMAIN
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — FAILURE-DOMAIN CONTRACT
# ============================================================================
#
# Recovery/routing may eventually require explicit knowledge of:
#
#
#       provider
#
#       region
#
#       cluster
#
#       gateway
#
#       network
#
#
# to avoid correlated failures.
#
#
# If so, failure domain may earn its own domain contract.
#
#
# Do not make endpoint.py carry a grab bag of:
#
#
#       cloud
#
#       region
#
#       cluster
#
#       zone
#
#       rack
#
#
# unless endpoint identity truly owns those facts.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FAILURE DOMAIN != ENDPOINT
# ============================================================================
#
# A network endpoint participates in a failure domain.
#
#
# It is not itself the complete failure-domain model.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — SERVICE DISCOVERY SYSTEMS
# ============================================================================
#
# Kubernetes may not remain the only infrastructure discovery source.
#
#
# Future organizations may use:
#
#
#       Kubernetes
#
#       Consul
#
#       cloud-native registries
#
#       service mesh control planes
#
#       internal deployment databases
#
#       CMDB/inventory systems
#
#
# EndpointEvidenceSource exists partly to protect Agent 11 from becoming tied
# to one of these systems.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# MANAGEMENT PLANE EXISTS != MANAGEMENT PLANE OWNS THE DOMAIN
# ============================================================================
#
# Fleet-management platforms may eventually provide organization-wide
# cluster/service views.
#
#
# Their existence does not automatically make them Agent 11's authoritative
# domain model.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — CLUSTER FLEETS
# ============================================================================
#
# Future infrastructure may include:
#
#
#       Kubernetes fleets
#
#       Azure Arc
#
#       cloud-specific multi-cluster management
#
#       internal platform engineering control planes
#
#
# These may be useful evidence sources.
#
#
# They remain infrastructure/control-plane concerns.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# CONTROL PLANE INVENTORY != CURRENT DATA-PLANE REACHABILITY
# ============================================================================
#
# A fleet system may report:
#
#
#       service exists in cluster C
#
#
# while Agent 11 has:
#
#
#       no viable network path to cluster C.
#
#
# Therefore:
#
#
#       DISCOVERED DEPLOYMENT
#           !=
#       REACHABLE DEPLOYMENT
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — DNS
# ============================================================================
#
# Endpoint representation may eventually depend heavily on DNS.
#
#
# But:
#
#
#       DNS NAME EXISTS
#
#
# does not establish:
#
#
#       DNS resolution succeeds
#
#       route exists
#
#       TLS succeeds
#
#       service responds
#
#
# ============================================================================
# ENDPOINT IDENTITY != DNS SUCCESS
# ============================================================================
#
# Keep DNS/path concerns out of endpoint existence unless the domain later
# proves they belong together.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — TLS
# ============================================================================
#
# A reachable endpoint may fail:
#
#
#       certificate validation
#
#       trust chain
#
#       hostname verification
#
#       mTLS authentication
#
#
# This is network/security-path evidence.
#
#
# It should not automatically become:
#
#
#       endpoint absent
#
#
# ============================================================================
# TLS FAILURE != ENDPOINT ABSENCE
# ============================================================================
#
# Preserve causal precision.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — NETWORKPOLICY
# ============================================================================
#
# Kubernetes NetworkPolicy may permit or deny traffic.
#
#
# Its effect belongs closer to:
#
#
#       path/reachability evaluation
#
#
# rather than endpoint discovery.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# NetworkPolicy != Agent 11 Policy
# ============================================================================
#
# Kubernetes NetworkPolicy says:
#
#
#       whether network traffic may flow
#
#
# under Kubernetes networking rules.
#
#
# Agent 11 policy says:
#
#
#       whether AI data may be sent to a destination.
#
#
# Both may need to permit the route.
#
#
# They remain different systems.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — BGP
# ============================================================================
#
# BGP may eventually influence how Agent 11 reaches:
#
#
#       clusters
#
#       cloud networks
#
#       private inference endpoints
#
#
# BGP belongs to network-path truth.
#
#
# It should not be interpreted by endpoint discovery.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# BGP ROUTE != ENDPOINT IDENTITY
# ============================================================================
#
# A route advertisement may appear or disappear while endpoint identity
# remains unchanged.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# BGP REACHABLE != AI AUTHORIZED
# ============================================================================
#
# Preserve forever.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — SD-WAN
# ============================================================================
#
# Same pattern.
#
#
# SD-WAN may influence:
#
#
#       path choice
#
#       path health
#
#       transport
#
#
# It does not define:
#
#
#       endpoint identity
#
#       model capability
#
#       Agent 11 policy
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — ENDPOINT HEALTH
# ============================================================================
#
# Do not allow:
#
#
#       endpoint exists
#
#
# to slowly evolve into:
#
#
#       endpoint exists and is healthy and reachable and serving inference.
#
#
# Those should remain separate evidence dimensions unless the architecture
# intentionally creates a richer aggregate.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# ENDPOINT.PY != HEALTH.PY
# ============================================================================
#
# If future endpoint.py begins performing:
#
#
#       inference probes
#
#       HTTP health checks
#
#       latency measurement
#
#       GPU inspection
#
#       queue-depth inspection
#
#
# then endpoint behavior has absorbed health behavior.
#
#
# Move the concern.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# ENDPOINT.PY != PATH.PY
# ============================================================================
#
# If future endpoint.py begins performing:
#
#
#       route-table inspection
#
#       BGP analysis
#
#       VPN inspection
#
#       DNS reachability
#
#       TLS path validation
#
#
# then endpoint behavior has absorbed path behavior.
#
#
# Move the concern.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# ENDPOINT.PY != POLICY.PY
# ============================================================================
#
# If future endpoint.py contains:
#
#
#       E7
#
#       E8
#
#       E9
#
#       UserDataPreference
#
#
# stop.
#
#
# Endpoint evidence does not own AI authorization.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# ENDPOINT.PY != ROUTER.PY
# ============================================================================
#
# If future endpoint.py decides:
#
#
#       which FM to use
#
#       which route wins
#
#       which candidate is preferred
#
#
# stop.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# ENDPOINT.PY != REMEDIATION ENGINE
# ============================================================================
#
# Endpoint discovery should not:
#
#
#       restart Pod
#
#       scale Deployment
#
#       modify Service
#
#       change Gateway
#
#       edit NetworkPolicy
#
#
# unless future architecture explicitly introduces a separate remediation
# authority.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# OBSERVE != REMEDIATE
# ============================================================================
#
# Preserve:
#
#
#       KNOWING SOMETHING IS WRONG
#           !=
#       AUTHORITY TO CHANGE PRODUCTION
#
#
# ============================================================================
# AI RECOMMENDATION != INFRASTRUCTURE AUTHORITY
# ============================================================================
#
# Agentic AI may eventually reason:
#
#
#       "Restart the inference deployment."
#
#
# That does not mean the endpoint-observation component possesses credentials
# to do so.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# KUBERNETES READ RBAC != KUBERNETES WRITE RBAC
# ============================================================================
#
# Future endpoint adapters should remain read-oriented wherever possible.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# KUBERNETES WRITE AUTHORITY SHOULD LIVE ELSEWHERE
# ============================================================================
#
# If remediation is later introduced, use:
#
#
#       separate credentials
#
#       separate policy
#
#       separate auditing
#
#       separate behavioral components
#
#
# rather than upgrading endpoint discovery to cluster-admin.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — ENDPOINT TRUST
# ============================================================================
#
# Endpoint existence is not trust.
#
#
# Future systems may need evidence for:
#
#
#       certificate identity
#
#       workload identity
#
#       service identity
#
#       mTLS
#
#
# But:
#
#
#       ENDPOINT DISCOVERED
#           !=
#       ENDPOINT TRUSTED
#
#
# ============================================================================
# TRUSTED ENDPOINT != AUTHORIZED AI DESTINATION
# ============================================================================
#
# Even a cryptographically authenticated endpoint may not be authorized for:
#
#
#       a particular classification
#
#       a particular user
#
#       a particular request
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — SERVICE MAPPING TRUST
# ============================================================================
#
# If Kubernetes labels map:
#
#
#       agent11.service-id
#
#
# to infrastructure:
#
#
#       WHO MAY WRITE THAT LABEL?
#
#
# becomes a security question.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# LABEL MUTATION MAY BECOME ROUTING CONTROL
# ============================================================================
#
# If changing a label changes which endpoint Agent 11 associates with a
# service, then label-write authority may effectively influence routing.
#
#
# This may require:
#
#
#       RBAC review
#
#       admission policy
#
#       audit
#
#       controlled deployment processes
#
#
# ============================================================================
# DISCOVERY METADATA CAN BECOME SECURITY-RELEVANT
# ============================================================================
#
# Just because metadata begins as:
#
#
#       "convenient discovery information"
#
#
# does not mean it remains low-risk once routing depends on it.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — EVIDENCE PROVENANCE
# ============================================================================
#
# If endpoint evidence becomes security-relevant, Future Agent 11 may need to
# know:
#
#
#       who produced the evidence
#
#       which cluster produced it
#
#       when it was observed
#
#       whether it was authenticated
#
#
# This may justify a future evidence contract.
#
#
# Do not create one until required.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# VALID DATA != TRUSTED EVIDENCE
# ============================================================================
#
# Pydantic may eventually prove:
#
#
#       field types are valid
#
#
# It does not prove:
#
#
#       evidence source is trustworthy
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — EVENTUAL CONSISTENCY
# ============================================================================
#
# Distributed infrastructure rarely changes everywhere simultaneously.
#
#
# Example:
#
#
#       Pod terminated
#
#       EndpointSlice update pending
#
#       local cache still shows endpoint
#
#       service mesh has already removed backend
#
#
# Different evidence sources may temporarily disagree.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# EVENTUAL CONSISTENCY != BAD DATA
# ============================================================================
#
# Temporary disagreement may be normal distributed-system behavior.
#
#
# Future network evidence evaluation may need to understand:
#
#
#       source
#
#       freshness
#
#       authority
#
#
# rather than simply selecting the newest Boolean.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — SOURCE PRECEDENCE
# ============================================================================
#
# If:
#
#
#       Kubernetes says ready
#
#       network probe says unreachable
#
#
# neither source necessarily overrides the other.
#
#
# They answer different questions.
#
#
# Therefore:
#
#
#       EVIDENCE PRECEDENCE
#
#
# may be the wrong abstraction.
#
#
# Instead:
#
#
#       preserve dimensions.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# PRESERVE DIMENSIONS
# ============================================================================
#
# Prefer:
#
#
#       endpoint_present = true
#
#       backend_ready = true
#
#       path_reachable = false
#
#
# conceptually,
#
# rather than:
#
#
#       overall_health = 0.66
#
#
# ============================================================================
# DO NOT AVERAGE SECURITY-RELEVANT FACTS
# ============================================================================
#
# Especially never:
#
#
#       endpoint 1.0
#
#       path 0.0
#
#       policy 1.0
#
#       average = 0.67
#
#       route allowed
#
#
# No.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# POLICY NEVER BECOMES AN INFRASTRUCTURE SCORE
# ============================================================================
#
# Preserve the same invariant across network evolution.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — ENDPOINT CAPACITY
# ============================================================================
#
# Endpoint presence may eventually correlate poorly with service capacity.
#
#
# Example:
#
#
#       4 ready Pods
#
#
# could mean:
#
#
#       excellent capacity
#
#
# or:
#
#
#       four saturated Pods.
#
#
# Capacity belongs to health/runtime behavior.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# READY COUNT != CAPACITY
# ============================================================================
#
# Preserve.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — ENDPOINT DRAINING
# ============================================================================
#
# Kubernetes may contain endpoints that are:
#
#
#       terminating
#
#       draining
#
#       not accepting new work
#
#
# Future adapters may need richer interpretation than:
#
#
#       present / absent
#
#
# ============================================================================
# TERMINATING != ABSENT
# ============================================================================
#
# But it may be:
#
#
#       unsuitable for new traffic.
#
#
# If this distinction becomes necessary:
#
#
#       evolve the evidence contract explicitly.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — TOPOLOGY-AWARE ROUTING
# ============================================================================
#
# Kubernetes and service meshes may expose topology information such as:
#
#
#       zone
#
#       region
#
#       node
#
#       cluster
#
#
# Agent 11 may eventually use some of that for:
#
#
#       latency
#
#       residency
#
#       failure-domain diversity
#
#
# But topology preference is not endpoint existence.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# TOPOLOGY != AUTHORIZATION
# ============================================================================
#
# A nearby endpoint is not automatically permitted.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — CROSS-CLUSTER SERVICE DISCOVERY
# ============================================================================
#
# Future infrastructure may provide one logical service across many clusters.
#
#
# Endpoint discovery may then become:
#
#
#       service
#           |
#           +--> cluster A endpoints
#           |
#           +--> cluster B endpoints
#           |
#           +--> cluster C endpoints
#
#
# A future aggregation layer may need to preserve cluster provenance.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# CROSS-CLUSTER DISCOVERY != CROSS-CLUSTER REACHABILITY
# ============================================================================
#
# Discovering an endpoint in another cluster does not establish a path to it.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# CROSS-CLUSTER REACHABILITY != POLICY AUTHORIZATION
# ============================================================================
#
# As always.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — CLOUD PRIVATE NETWORKING
# ============================================================================
#
# Multi-cloud AI may involve:
#
#
#       VPC peering
#
#       VNet peering
#
#       transit gateways
#
#       cloud interconnect
#
#       private service endpoints
#
#       SD-WAN
#
#       VPN
#
#
# These belong to network-path behavior.
#
#
# Endpoint discovery should merely identify the network destination evidence
# required by path evaluation.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — EXTERNAL FOUNDATION MODELS
# ============================================================================
#
# Not every endpoint will be Kubernetes-backed.
#
#
# External FMs may expose provider-managed endpoints outside Agent 11's
# Kubernetes control.
#
#
# EndpointEvidenceSource therefore must remain infrastructure-neutral.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# KUBERNETES ADAPTER != UNIVERSAL ENDPOINT ADAPTER
# ============================================================================
#
# Future providers may require:
#
#
#       provider API adapter
#
#       static endpoint source
#
#       internal registry source
#
#
# alongside Kubernetes.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — PROPRIETARY MODELS
# ============================================================================
#
# Acquired or company-developed models may live:
#
#
#       inside GKE
#
#       inside AKS
#
#       inside EKS
#
#       inside on-prem Kubernetes
#
#       outside Kubernetes entirely
#
#
# Endpoint architecture must remain neutral to model origin.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# MODEL ORIGIN != ENDPOINT TYPE
# ============================================================================
#
# A proprietary model is not automatically:
#
#
#       on-prem
#
#       private
#
#       Kubernetes
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — MULTI-FM SERVICES
# ============================================================================
#
# A future gateway service may expose several underlying models.
#
#
# Then:
#
#
#       one network endpoint
#
#
# may front:
#
#
#       multiple logical AI models.
#
#
# This is another reason:
#
#
#       ENDPOINT != MODEL
#
#
# ============================================================================
# ONE ENDPOINT MAY FRONT MANY SERVICES
# ============================================================================
#
# API gateways and model gateways may multiplex:
#
#
#       Service A
#
#       Service B
#
#       Service C
#
#
# through one network destination.
#
#
# Future mapping may therefore become many-to-one or one-to-many.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# SERVICE -> ENDPOINT MAY NOT REMAIN ONE-TO-ONE
# ============================================================================
#
# SEIR-I should not encode a hard one-to-one invariant unless the actual
# domain requires it.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — MODEL GATEWAYS
# ============================================================================
#
# A future internal model gateway may dynamically route:
#
#
#       Claude
#
#       Gemini
#
#       proprietary models
#
#
# behind one endpoint.
#
#
# At that point Agent 11 must understand whether:
#
#
#       model selection happens before gateway invocation
#
#
# or:
#
#
#       gateway performs model selection.
#
#
# That is not an endpoint.py decision.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# NETWORK ENDPOINT != MODEL-SELECTION BOUNDARY
# ============================================================================
#
# Preserve.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — GATEWAY HEALTH
# ============================================================================
#
# A gateway endpoint may be healthy while:
#
#
#       one backend model is unavailable
#
#
# Therefore:
#
#
#       GATEWAY HEALTH
#           !=
#       BACKEND MODEL HEALTH
#
#
# Another reason to preserve layers.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — AUTHENTICATION ENDPOINTS
# ============================================================================
#
# Some inference paths may depend on:
#
#
#       token service
#
#       identity provider
#
#       certificate authority
#
#
# Those dependencies may fail even while inference endpoint exists.
#
#
# Endpoint discovery should not attempt to model every dependency.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# DEPENDENCY GRAPH != ENDPOINT MODEL
# ============================================================================
#
# If dependency topology becomes necessary for resilience, it may deserve a
# separate architecture.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — ENDPOINT SECURITY POSTURE
# ============================================================================
#
# Future systems may wish to describe:
#
#
#       public/private
#
#       TLS
#
#       mTLS
#
#       identity
#
#       network segmentation
#
#
# Be cautious.
#
#
# Some facts may belong to endpoint description.
#
# Others may belong to path security.
#
# Others may belong to policy.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# SECURITY POSTURE != AUTHORIZATION DECISION
# ============================================================================
#
# Even excellent transport security does not authorize prohibited routing.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — NETWORK ORCHESTRATOR
# ============================================================================
#
# As endpoint, health, and path evidence mature, a network orchestrator may
# eventually coordinate:
#
#
#       endpoint evidence
#
#       health evidence
#
#       path evidence
#
#
# and produce a higher-level network result.
#
#
# But:
#
#
#       ORCHESTRATOR
#           =
#       COORDINATION
#
#
# not:
#
#
#       ORCHESTRATOR
#           =
#       ALL NETWORK LOGIC
#
#
# ============================================================================
# DO NOT BUILD NetworkOrchestrator YET
# ============================================================================
#
# Endpoint, health, and path responsibilities should be designed first.
#
#
# Only then can orchestration be defined from actual collaborators.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — NETWORK CONTEXT
# ============================================================================
#
# This entire endpoint design is being done before returning to:
#
#
#       routing/network_context.py
#
#
# That is intentional.
#
#
# Once endpoint, health, and path semantics exist, we can determine whether:
#
#
#       routing/network_context.py
#
#
# actually has a responsibility.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# NETWORK CONTEXT MAY DISAPPEAR
# ============================================================================
#
# If the network subsystem eventually produces exactly the routing-relevant
# reachability evidence required by candidate evaluation:
#
#
#       routing/network_context.py
#
#
# may be unnecessary.
#
#
# Deleting a planned file is acceptable.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# INITIAL DIRECTORY TREE != ARCHITECTURAL LAW
# ============================================================================
#
# Preserve.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — CANDIDATE EVALUATOR
# ============================================================================
#
# Endpoint evidence eventually contributes to:
#
#
#       network reachability
#
#
# which eventually contributes to:
#
#
#       RoutingCandidate
#
#
# But endpoint.py should not create RoutingCandidate.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# ENDPOINT EVIDENCE != ROUTING CANDIDATE
# ============================================================================
#
# Endpoint is only one piece of the network side of routing viability.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — HOW MUCH EVIDENCE SHOULD ROUTING SEE?
# ============================================================================
#
# Routing likely should not need:
#
#
#       EndpointSlice objects
#
#       Pod names
#
#       Gateway resources
#
#       cloud load-balancer IDs
#
#
# Routing should consume normalized Agent 11 evidence.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# ROUTING SHOULD NOT KNOW KUBERNETES
# ============================================================================
#
# If future routing code imports:
#
#
#       kubernetes.client
#
#
# stop.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# POLICY SHOULD NOT KNOW KUBERNETES INTERNALS
# ============================================================================
#
# Policy might eventually consume trusted attributes such as:
#
#
#       region
#
#       deployment class
#
#       exposure type
#
#
# But policy should not need:
#
#
#       V1EndpointSlice
#
#       Pod UID
#
#       kube context
#
#
# ============================================================================
# DOMAIN ATTRIBUTES MAY BE DERIVED FROM INFRASTRUCTURE
# ============================================================================
#
# Future adapters may translate infrastructure into trusted domain facts.
#
#
# Example:
#
#
#       Kubernetes cluster metadata
#           ->
#       deployment region
#
#
# But the trust and provenance of that translation must be explicit if it
# becomes security-relevant.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — TESTING MULTI-CLUSTER
# ============================================================================
#
# Tests should eventually distinguish:
#
#
#       endpoint semantics
#
#       Kubernetes translation
#
#       multi-cluster aggregation
#
#       path evaluation
#
#
# Do not build one enormous integration test as the only proof of correctness.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# TEST BOUNDARIES INDIVIDUALLY
# ============================================================================
#
# Future tests might independently verify:
#
#
#       Service -> EndpointSlice interpretation
#
#       cluster A source
#
#       cluster B source
#
#       aggregation
#
#       stale evidence
#
#       API failure
#
#       readiness transitions
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — CHAOS
# ============================================================================
#
# Mature infrastructure may eventually justify controlled tests such as:
#
#
#       kill Pod
#
#       remove endpoint
#
#       disconnect Kubernetes API
#
#       break cluster route
#
#       isolate one region
#
#
# The purpose is to verify evidence semantics and recovery.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# CHAOS TESTING != PRODUCTION AUTHORITY
# ============================================================================
#
# Chaos tooling requires its own permissions and safety boundaries.
#
#
# Endpoint observation should not acquire mutation authority to make chaos
# testing convenient.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — ENDPOINT EVENT HISTORY
# ============================================================================
#
# Telemetry may eventually preserve:
#
#
#       endpoint discovered
#
#       endpoint ready
#
#       endpoint removed
#
#       endpoint source unavailable
#
#
# Event history may help incident analysis.
#
#
# That does not mean event history belongs inside NetworkEndpointEvaluator.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# CURRENT STATE != EVENT HISTORY
# ============================================================================
#
# Preserve.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — TELEMETRY
# ============================================================================
#
# Endpoint telemetry may eventually include:
#
#
#       service_id
#
#       cluster identity
#
#       source type
#
#       endpoint count
#
#       ready count
#
#       observation time
#
#       discovery errors
#
#
# But telemetry needs should not automatically define domain-model fields.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# TELEMETRY != DOMAIN CONTRACT
# ============================================================================
#
# Preserve.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — AUDIT
# ============================================================================
#
# If endpoint mappings influence security-sensitive routing, audit may need
# to preserve:
#
#
#       service-to-infrastructure mapping changes
#
#       who changed metadata
#
#       which endpoint source produced the decision
#
#
# Again:
#
#
#       AUDIT REQUIREMENT
#
#
# may justify future provenance contracts.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — SUPPLY-CHAIN TRUST
# ============================================================================
#
# Kubernetes infrastructure itself may be compromised.
#
#
# Therefore:
#
#
#       KUBERNETES API SAYS X
#
#
# is not a metaphysical guarantee that X is safe.
#
#
# Future security architecture may require:
#
#
#       control-plane trust
#
#       workload identity
#
#       admission policy
#
#       image provenance
#
#
# But endpoint.py should not become the supply-chain security subsystem.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# ENDPOINT DISCOVERY != SUPPLY-CHAIN ASSURANCE
# ============================================================================
#
# Preserve.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — POLICY USING ENDPOINT ATTRIBUTES
# ============================================================================
#
# Policy may eventually care about:
#
#
#       private vs public
#
#       region
#
#       organization ownership
#
#       deployment environment
#
#
# If so, these attributes must become trusted domain facts.
#
#
# Do not let policy parse Kubernetes objects directly.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# POLICY SHOULD CONSUME DOMAIN FACTS
# ============================================================================
#
# Good future direction:
#
#
#       Kubernetes
#           |
#           v
#       trusted translation
#           |
#           v
#       Agent 11 deployment/endpoint facts
#           |
#           v
#       Policy
#
#
# not:
#
#
#       Policy
#           |
#           v
#       parse V1Service annotations
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — ENDPOINT EVIDENCE OBJECT
# ============================================================================
#
# The current Protocol returns:
#
#
#       bool
#
#
# Future operational complexity may eventually justify:
#
#
#       EndpointEvidence
#
#
# containing explicit state/provenance.
#
#
# But do not create it merely because this comment mentions the name.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# NAME IN COMMENT != ARCHITECTURAL COMMITMENT
# ============================================================================
#
# Preserve.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# IF bool BECOMES TOO SMALL
# ============================================================================
#
# Evidence that the bool has outgrown the architecture might include a need
# to distinguish:
#
#
#       PRESENT
#
#       ABSENT
#
#       UNKNOWN
#
#       STALE
#
#       OBSERVATION_ERROR
#
#
# or to preserve:
#
#
#       source
#
#       observed_at
#
#       endpoint count
#
#
# At that point:
#
#
#       evolve the contract deliberately.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# DO NOT ADD OPTIONAL FIELDS UNTIL bool LOOKS LIKE AN AIRCRAFT COCKPIT
# ============================================================================
#
# Avoid:
#
#
#       has_endpoint(...)
#
#       last_error=None
#
#       maybe_stale=False
#
#       source=None
#
#       confidence=None
#
#
# accumulating as unrelated side channels.
#
#
# If richer evidence exists:
#
#
#       CREATE A COHERENT CONTRACT.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — CONFIDENCE
# ============================================================================
#
# Be cautious about:
#
#
#       confidence: float
#
#
# Confidence in what?
#
#
#       endpoint existence?
#
#       freshness?
#
#       source trust?
#
#
# A generic number would likely hide more than it explains.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — SOURCE TRUST
# ============================================================================
#
# Different evidence sources may have different authority.
#
#
# Example:
#
#
#       Kubernetes API
#
#       local cache
#
#       service mesh
#
#       CMDB
#
#
# Future network evidence may need explicit trust semantics.
#
#
# But:
#
#
#       TRUST != CONFIDENCE SCORE
#
#
# necessarily.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — SECURITY OF CACHES
# ============================================================================
#
# If endpoint evidence is cached:
#
#
#       cache tampering
#
#       stale cache
#
#       cross-tenant cache
#
#
# could influence routing.
#
#
# The cache may therefore become security-relevant infrastructure.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# CACHED EVIDENCE != AUTHORITATIVE FOREVER
# ============================================================================
#
# Preserve.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — TENANCY
# ============================================================================
#
# Multi-tenant platforms may expose:
#
#
#       same service name
#
#       different tenant
#
#       different namespace
#
#       different cluster
#
#
# Endpoint identity may eventually require tenant scope.
#
#
# Do not add tenant_id until Agent 11 actually needs this.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — ENVIRONMENTS
# ============================================================================
#
# Organizations may have:
#
#
#       development
#
#       staging
#
#       production
#
#
# with similar names.
#
#
# Endpoint discovery must not accidentally map:
#
#
#       production AIService
#
#
# to:
#
#
#       development Kubernetes Service
#
#
# ============================================================================
# ENVIRONMENT IDENTITY MAY BECOME SECURITY-RELEVANT
# ============================================================================
#
# If mappings influence routing, environment boundaries matter.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — CROSS-ENVIRONMENT FALLBACK
# ============================================================================
#
# Never assume:
#
#
#       prod failed
#           ->
#       use dev
#
#
# merely because a development endpoint exists.
#
#
# That would be a policy/deployment decision.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — NAMESPACE AS ENVIRONMENT
# ============================================================================
#
# Some organizations encode environments through namespaces.
#
#
# Others use:
#
#       separate clusters
#
#       separate accounts
#
#       separate subscriptions
#
#
# Therefore:
#
#
#       namespace
#           !=
#       environment
#
#
# universally.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — ZERO TRUST
# ============================================================================
#
# A future organization may use zero-trust networking around Kubernetes AI
# services.
#
#
# Endpoint evidence remains:
#
#
#       destination evidence
#
#
# not:
#
#
#       proof that every zero-trust authorization gate will succeed.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# NETWORK ACCESS != AI POLICY
# ============================================================================
#
# Preserve again because it will keep trying to merge.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — MODEL ENDPOINT HOT-SWAP
# ============================================================================
#
# A gateway may keep the same endpoint while changing:
#
#
#       model version
#
#       model family
#
#       deployment
#
#
# Therefore:
#
#
#       ENDPOINT STABLE
#           !=
#       MODEL STABLE
#
#
# ============================================================================
# ENDPOINT IDENTITY != MODEL VERSION
# ============================================================================
#
# Preserve.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — BLUE/GREEN DEPLOYMENT
# ============================================================================
#
# Future infrastructure may run:
#
#
#       blue model deployment
#
#       green model deployment
#
#
# behind:
#
#
#       same service endpoint
#
#
# Agent 11 endpoint evidence may not reveal which model instance ultimately
# receives traffic.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# ENDPOINT RESOLUTION MAY HIDE DEPLOYMENT SELECTION
# ============================================================================
#
# If that matters to policy/assurance:
#
#
#       deployment selection must become explicit
#
#
# somewhere above/below the endpoint layer.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — CANARY TRAFFIC
# ============================================================================
#
# Kubernetes/service mesh may route some traffic to:
#
#
#       experimental model deployment.
#
#
# If Agent 11 requires deterministic model assurance, infrastructure-level
# traffic splitting could conflict with AI model-selection semantics.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# INFRASTRUCTURE TRAFFIC SPLIT != AGENT 11 MODEL SELECTION
# ============================================================================
#
# This may eventually require coordination between:
#
#
#       model registry
#
#       deployment configuration
#
#       service mesh
#
#       routing
#
#
# not endpoint.py alone.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — SHADOW TRAFFIC
# ============================================================================
#
# Some platforms send production traffic copies to shadow deployments.
#
#
# This has serious implications for:
#
#
#       data disclosure
#
#       privacy
#
#       model qualification
#
#
# Agent 11 policy may need awareness.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# ONE SELECTED ENDPOINT != ONE DATA RECIPIENT
# ============================================================================
#
# If infrastructure mirrors traffic:
#
#
#       selected service
#
#
# may not equal:
#
#
#       only system receiving data.
#
#
# This is a major future policy concern.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# ENDPOINT.PY CANNOT SOLVE TRAFFIC MIRRORING POLICY
# ============================================================================
#
# Preserve.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — AUDIT THE ACTUAL DESTINATION
# ============================================================================
#
# If gateways/meshes dynamically route requests, audit may eventually need to
# record:
#
#
#       logical selected service
#
#       actual deployment
#
#       actual endpoint
#
#       actual model version
#
#
# if available.
#
#
# This may create a distinction between:
#
#
#       intended route
#
#       realized route
#
#
# ============================================================================
# INTENDED DESTINATION != REALIZED DESTINATION
# ============================================================================
#
# Distributed routing layers can introduce this distinction.
#
#
# If it matters to security/compliance, Future Agent 11 may need explicit
# evidence.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — WHO OWNS REALIZED DESTINATION EVIDENCE?
# ============================================================================
#
# Possibly:
#
#
#       service mesh telemetry
#
#       gateway telemetry
#
#       inference gateway
#
#       model runtime
#
#
# Again:
#
#
#       endpoint discovery
#
#
# is not automatically the owner.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — IPV4 / IPV6
# ============================================================================
#
# Do not define endpoint identity around one address family unless required.
#
#
# Modern clusters may expose:
#
#
#       IPv4
#
#       IPv6
#
#       dual-stack
#
#
# Endpoint identity should remain abstract enough to survive networking
# evolution.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — PORTS
# ============================================================================
#
# One endpoint may expose:
#
#
#       HTTPS
#
#       gRPC
#
#       metrics
#
#       admin interfaces
#
#
# Agent 11 must not infer:
#
#
#       endpoint address exists
#
# therefore:
#
#       inference port is usable.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# PORT EXISTS != INFERENCE SERVICE AVAILABLE
# ============================================================================
#
# Preserve.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — PROTOCOL
# ============================================================================
#
# Endpoint evidence may eventually need to distinguish:
#
#
#       HTTPS
#
#       gRPC
#
#       WebSocket
#
#       streaming HTTP
#
#
# But protocol belongs in the endpoint contract only if behavior actually
# needs it.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — STREAMING
# ============================================================================
#
# Streaming inference may have different path requirements than ordinary
# request/response traffic.
#
#
# That probably belongs to:
#
#       path suitability
#
# rather than:
#
#       endpoint existence.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — LOCALITY
# ============================================================================
#
# Endpoint locality may matter for:
#
#
#       latency
#
#       cost
#
#       residency
#
#       failure domains
#
#
# But:
#
#
#       LOCAL != AUTHORIZED
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — REGION
# ============================================================================
#
# Region may eventually become a deployment property rather than endpoint
# identity.
#
#
# Avoid duplicating it across many models unless needed.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — ZONE
# ============================================================================
#
# Zone may matter for failure domains.
#
#
# Again:
#
#
#       topology metadata
#
#
# is not automatically endpoint identity.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — ENDPOINT OWNERSHIP
# ============================================================================
#
# Future policy may care whether an endpoint belongs to:
#
#
#       company
#
#       external provider
#
#       acquired company
#
#       third party
#
#
# Ownership is likely a deployment/service/policy fact.
#
#
# Do not infer ownership from DNS suffixes.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# DNS SUFFIX != TRUST
# ============================================================================
#
# Preserve.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — ENDPOINT SECURITY SCANNING
# ============================================================================
#
# Vulnerability scanning or certificate inspection may eventually produce
# endpoint-related evidence.
#
#
# That belongs to security/assurance behavior.
#
#
# NetworkEndpointEvaluator should not become a scanner.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — RATE LIMITS
# ============================================================================
#
# Endpoint exists and is healthy while:
#
#
#       provider quota exhausted
#
#       gateway rate limit reached
#
#
# Again:
#
#
#       ENDPOINT EVIDENCE != CAPACITY
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — LOAD BALANCER HEALTH
# ============================================================================
#
# Cloud load balancer health may differ from:
#
#
#       Kubernetes readiness
#
#       application inference health
#
#       caller reachability
#
#
# Preserve the dimensions.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — EXTERNAL DNS / GLOBAL LOAD BALANCERS
# ============================================================================
#
# One hostname may dynamically resolve to:
#
#
#       several regions
#
#       several providers
#
#       several clusters
#
#
# Endpoint identity may become global while realized endpoint varies.
#
#
# Another reason not to equate:
#
#
#       hostname
#
# with:
#
#       physical deployment.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — TRAFFIC MANAGERS
# ============================================================================
#
# DNS traffic managers, global accelerators, and cloud front doors may perform
# failover beneath Agent 11.
#
#
# Future architecture must understand whether:
#
#
#       infrastructure failover
#
#
# is transparent and policy-equivalent.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# TRANSPARENT FAILOVER MAY NOT BE POLICY-EQUIVALENT
# ============================================================================
#
# If traffic silently moves:
#
#
#       US region
#
#           ->
#       another jurisdiction
#
#
# policy/residency may care.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# INFRASTRUCTURE TRANSPARENCY != SECURITY TRANSPARENCY
# ============================================================================
#
# Preserve this one.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — SHOULD ENDPOINT EVIDENCE INCLUDE JURISDICTION?
# ============================================================================
#
# Maybe.
#
#
# Or jurisdiction may belong to deployment.
#
#
# Decide based on ownership and reuse.
#
#
# Do not scatter security-significant attributes across unrelated contracts.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — RECONCILIATION
# ============================================================================
#
# If endpoint sources disagree:
#
#
#       Kubernetes
#
#       cloud API
#
#       mesh telemetry
#
#
# a future reconciliation component may become necessary.
#
#
# Do not hide reconciliation inside:
#
#
#       has_endpoint(...)
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — CACHING
# ============================================================================
#
# If endpoint evidence is expensive to retrieve, caching may be useful.
#
#
# But cache architecture must answer:
#
#
#       who owns the cache?
#
#       how long is evidence valid?
#
#       what happens on source failure?
#
#       what happens after cluster failover?
#
#
# ============================================================================
# CACHE != SOURCE OF TRUTH
# ============================================================================
#
# Unless architecture explicitly defines it that way.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — EVENT-DRIVEN EVIDENCE
# ============================================================================
#
# Kubernetes watch events may eventually feed:
#
#
#       endpoint evidence cache
#
#
# This may allow:
#
#
#       O(1) local reads
#
#
# for endpoint evaluation.
#
#
# But event processing introduces:
#
#
#       ordering
#
#       missed events
#
#       watch restart
#
#       snapshot reconciliation
#
#
# ============================================================================
# EVENT STREAM != PERFECT TRUTH
# ============================================================================
#
# Future adapters may need periodic reconciliation.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — RUNTIME LIBRARY
# ============================================================================
#
# If Agent 11 eventually runs in multiple processes, endpoint evidence may need
# to become:
#
#
#       shared service
#
#       cache
#
#       control-plane API
#
#
# rather than in-process objects.
#
#
# That is an architectural evolution.
#
#
# Endpoint semantics should remain stable across process boundaries.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# PROCESS BOUNDARY != DOMAIN CHANGE
# ============================================================================
#
# Preserve.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — DISTRIBUTED EVIDENCE TRUST
# ============================================================================
#
# If endpoint evidence crosses process/network boundaries:
#
#
#       authentication
#
#       integrity
#
#       replay
#
#       freshness
#
#
# become security concerns.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# VALIDATED JSON != TRUSTED NETWORK EVIDENCE
# ============================================================================
#
# Preserve.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — API CONTRACTS
# ============================================================================
#
# If endpoint evidence becomes a network service, API schema versioning may
# eventually matter.
#
#
# Do not add version fields until there is a real compatibility boundary.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — MODEL OF UNKNOWN
# ============================================================================
#
# As infrastructure complexity grows,:
#
#
#       unknown
#
#       stale
#
#       unavailable
#
#       absent
#
#
# may need to become distinct states.
#
#
# This may be the point at which:
#
#
#       bool
#
#
# no longer suffices.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# UNKNOWN != ABSENT
# ============================================================================
#
# Preserve forever.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# STALE != ABSENT
# ============================================================================
#
# Preserve.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# DISCOVERY ERROR != ABSENT
# ============================================================================
#
# Preserve.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# ROUTING CONSEQUENCE MAY BE CONSERVATIVE
# ============================================================================
#
# Agent 11 may choose not to route when evidence is:
#
#
#       UNKNOWN
#
#       STALE
#
#
# while still preserving the actual evidence state.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FAIL CLOSED WITHOUT LYING
# ============================================================================
#
# Again, because infrastructure complexity will tempt people to collapse
# states.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — PERFORMANCE
# ============================================================================
#
# Endpoint lookup may eventually sit on every AI request.
#
#
# Future implementation may need:
#
#
#       local cache
#
#       indexed mapping
#
#       watch-based updates
#
#
# But optimize only after observing actual performance needs.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FAST != CORRECT
# ============================================================================
#
# A stale fast answer may be worse than a slightly slower correct one.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — CONSISTENCY MODEL
# ============================================================================
#
# Future network evidence may need explicit expectations:
#
#
#       strongly current
#
#       eventually consistent
#
#       bounded staleness
#
#
# But endpoint.py should not invent distributed-systems guarantees that its
# evidence sources cannot provide.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — POLICY REQUIRES FRESHER EVIDENCE
# ============================================================================
#
# Some sensitive routes may eventually require:
#
#
#       fresher network evidence
#
#
# than ordinary routes.
#
#
# If so:
#
#
#       freshness requirement
#
#
# may become a policy/candidate-evaluation concern rather than endpoint
# semantics.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# ENDPOINT OBSERVATION FRESHNESS != ROUTING FRESHNESS REQUIREMENT
# ============================================================================
#
# Preserve.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — REMOTE CLUSTER LATENCY
# ============================================================================
#
# Multi-cluster observation may itself become slow.
#
#
# That may favor:
#
#
#       local agents
#
#       cached fleet state
#
#       regional network observers
#
#
# rather than one central process querying every cluster synchronously.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# CENTRALIZED DISCOVERY != REQUIRED ARCHITECTURE
# ============================================================================
#
# Keep the domain contract independent from the observation topology.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — REGIONAL NETWORK AGENTS
# ============================================================================
#
# A future organization may deploy network observers near:
#
#
#       Tokyo
#
#       Virginia
#
#       Europe
#
#
# because reachability is observer-relative.
#
#
# This becomes especially important for:
#
#
#       path.py
#
#
# rather than endpoint identity itself.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# ENDPOINT EXISTENCE MAY BE GLOBAL
# ============================================================================
#
# while:
#
#
#       REACHABILITY
#
#
# is often caller-relative.
#
#
# Another reason to keep endpoint and path separate.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — CALLER IDENTITY
# ============================================================================
#
# Agent 11 instance A may reach an endpoint that Agent 11 instance B cannot.
#
#
# Endpoint existence should not silently incorporate caller-specific path
# truth.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# ENDPOINT EXISTS != CALLER CAN REACH IT
# ============================================================================
#
# Preserve.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — KUBERNETES API VERSION CHANGES
# ============================================================================
#
# Kubernetes APIs evolve.
#
#
# Infrastructure adapters may need updating.
#
#
# Agent 11 endpoint semantics should not change merely because:
#
#
#       Kubernetes SDK changed object shape.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# SDK VERSION != DOMAIN VERSION
# ============================================================================
#
# Preserve.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — CRD-BASED AI RUNTIMES
# ============================================================================
#
# Future AI platforms may expose models using Kubernetes custom resources.
#
#
# Examples may include:
#
#
#       inference services
#
#       model deployments
#
#       custom gateway resources
#
#
# Those remain infrastructure representations.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# CRD != AGENT 11 DOMAIN MODEL
# ============================================================================
#
# Unless the domain deliberately adopts the concept.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — KUBERNETES OPERATORS
# ============================================================================
#
# Operators may reconcile:
#
#
#       model deployment
#
#       service endpoint
#
#       scaling
#
#
# Endpoint discovery should observe resulting state.
#
#
# It should not duplicate operator reconciliation logic.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# OBSERVER != CONTROLLER
# ============================================================================
#
# This distinction is very important.
#
#
#       Kubernetes controller/operator
#
#           desired state -> actual state
#
#
#       Agent 11 endpoint observer
#
#           actual infrastructure -> evidence
#
#
# ============================================================================
# Agent 11 ENDPOINT EVALUATOR SHOULD NOT BECOME A K8S CONTROLLER
# ============================================================================
#
# If it starts reconciling desired state:
#
#
#       wrong component.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — IoC WITH MULTIPLE IMPLEMENTATIONS
# ============================================================================
#
# EndpointEvidenceSource may eventually have:
#
#
#       StaticEndpointEvidenceSource
#
#       KubernetesEndpointEvidenceSource
#
#       CloudEndpointEvidenceSource
#
#       RegistryEndpointEvidenceSource
#
#
# Application composition may select based upon deployment.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# SERVICE LOCATOR IS NOT REQUIRED
# ============================================================================
#
# Even with many implementations, avoid hidden global lookup unless it has a
# real architecture need.
#
#
# Explicit composition remains easier to reason about.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — COMPOSITE EVIDENCE SOURCE
# ============================================================================
#
# Eventually a source may need to combine:
#
#
#       Kubernetes
#
#       cloud API
#
#       static registry
#
#
# If so, consider a dedicated composite/aggregation component.
#
#
# Do not place source-combination logic inside NetworkEndpointEvaluator.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# EVALUATOR != AGGREGATOR
# ============================================================================
#
# Preserve unless evidence proves otherwise.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — PRIORITY OF SOURCES
# ============================================================================
#
# If several sources describe the same service:
#
#
#       WHICH ONE IS AUTHORITATIVE?
#
#
# That may require explicit trust semantics rather than arbitrary ordering.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FIRST SOURCE WINS != TRUST MODEL
# ============================================================================
#
# Preserve.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — ENDPOINT OWNERSHIP CHANGES
# ============================================================================
#
# Acquisitions or platform migrations may move a service:
#
#
#       GCP -> Azure
#
#       on-prem -> cloud
#
#
# while:
#
#
#       service_id remains stable.
#
#
# This validates keeping service identity separate from infrastructure
# endpoint identity.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# SERVICE IDENTITY SHOULD SURVIVE INFRASTRUCTURE MIGRATION
# ============================================================================
#
# Ideally.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — MIGRATION PERIODS
# ============================================================================
#
# During migration:
#
#
#       old endpoint
#
#       new endpoint
#
#
# may coexist.
#
#
# Future routing may need to know:
#
#
#       primary
#
#       migration
#
#       canary
#
#
# But endpoint existence alone should not choose one.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# ENDPOINT EVIDENCE != ENDPOINT PREFERENCE
# ============================================================================
#
# Preserve.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — DECOMMISSIONING
# ============================================================================
#
# A service may still have infrastructure artifacts while being logically
# retired.
#
#
# Therefore:
#
#
#       ENDPOINT EXISTS
#           !=
#       SERVICE ACTIVE
#
#
# Service lifecycle belongs elsewhere.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — ORPHANED INFRASTRUCTURE
# ============================================================================
#
# Kubernetes may contain:
#
#
#       orphaned Service
#
#       stale EndpointSlice
#
#       old Gateway route
#
#
# Discovery must not automatically make orphaned infrastructure an Agent 11
# service.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# INFRASTRUCTURE EXISTS != AGENT 11 SERVICE EXISTS
# ============================================================================
#
# Preserve.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — SECURITY INCIDENT RESPONSE
# ============================================================================
#
# During an incident, Security may need to:
#
#
#       quarantine endpoint
#
#       remove service mapping
#
#       disable route
#
#
# Those actions should occur through explicit policy/runtime mechanisms.
#
#
# Endpoint discovery should not invent quarantine semantics.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# QUARANTINED != ABSENT
# ============================================================================
#
# A quarantined endpoint may physically exist.
#
#
# It is simply not permitted/usable under current security controls.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — OBSERVABILITY LABELS
# ============================================================================
#
# Prometheus/service-mesh labels may eventually help correlate endpoint
# telemetry.
#
#
# Those labels are telemetry metadata.
#
#
# They should not become endpoint identity unless intentionally designed.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — PROMETHEUS
# ============================================================================
#
# Metrics may eventually inform:
#
#
#       service health
#
#       capacity
#
#       latency
#
#
# This belongs more naturally in:
#
#
#       health.py
#
#
# not endpoint existence.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — OPEN TELEMETRY
# ============================================================================
#
# Same general rule.
#
#
# Traces may help identify:
#
#
#       realized endpoint
#
#       latency
#
#       failures
#
#
# But telemetry is evidence, not endpoint identity itself.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# TELEMETRY != ENDPOINT DEFINITION
# ============================================================================
#
# Preserve.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — API GATEWAYS
# ============================================================================
#
# Agent 11 may invoke:
#
#
#       one API gateway endpoint
#
#
# that proxies many model services.
#
#
# Endpoint presence may therefore become detached from individual model
# services.
#
#
# This may require mapping:
#
#
#       AIService
#
#       route/path within gateway
#
#       endpoint
#
#
# ============================================================================
# URL PATH != NETWORK ENDPOINT NECESSARILY
# ============================================================================
#
# Depending upon the domain.
#
#
# Do not prematurely model:
#
#
#       endpoint = full URL
#
#
# without considering gateways.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — PORTABLE ENDPOINT IDENTIFIERS
# ============================================================================
#
# If endpoint objects become persisted, identifiers should not depend too
# heavily on volatile:
#
#
#       Pod name
#
#       Pod IP
#
#       ephemeral resource
#
#
# unless the endpoint specifically represents an ephemeral backend.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# EPHEMERAL BACKEND != STABLE SERVICE ENDPOINT
# ============================================================================
#
# Preserve.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — POD IPS
# ============================================================================
#
# Pod IPs are often ephemeral.
#
#
# Therefore:
#
#
#       POD IP
#
#
# is rarely a good stable Agent 11 endpoint identity by itself.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — ENDPOINTSLICE ADDRESS
# ============================================================================
#
# EndpointSlice addresses may similarly represent transient backend state.
#
#
# Treat them as infrastructure evidence unless Future Agent 11 truly needs
# backend-level identity.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — STATEFUL MODEL SERVERS
# ============================================================================
#
# Some future AI workloads may maintain:
#
#
#       session affinity
#
#       cache state
#
#       KV cache
#
#       conversation state
#
#
# Then endpoint switching may have semantic consequences.
#
#
# That belongs to service/runtime behavior, not simple endpoint existence.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# SAME SERVICE != INTERCHANGEABLE BACKEND
# ============================================================================
#
# Potential future concern.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — SESSION AFFINITY
# ============================================================================
#
# Kubernetes/service mesh may route a session consistently.
#
#
# Agent 11 fallback might disrupt it.
#
#
# That belongs to later routing/recovery architecture.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# ENDPOINT DISCOVERY SHOULD NOT INFER SESSION SAFETY
# ============================================================================
#
# Preserve.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — ZERO-DOWNTIME MODEL ROLLOUTS
# ============================================================================
#
# During rollout, multiple model versions may be behind one endpoint.
#
#
# Endpoint presence says nothing about:
#
#
#       exact model version served to a given request.
#
#
# If version assurance matters:
#
#
#       runtime/deployment evidence must become richer.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — POLICY REQUIRES EXACT MODEL VERSION
# ============================================================================
#
# Then infrastructure traffic splitting may need to be constrained.
#
#
# Again:
#
#
#       endpoint.py is not the policy engine.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — ENDPOINT CONTRACT IMMUTABILITY
# ============================================================================
#
# If first-class endpoint contracts eventually appear, prefer treating an
# observed endpoint snapshot as evidence rather than endlessly mutating one
# object across time.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# STATE EVOLUTION != HISTORY REWRITING
# ============================================================================
#
# Same principle established in routing fallback.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — ENDPOINT HISTORY
# ============================================================================
#
# Historical endpoint topology may help explain incidents.
#
#
# That probably belongs to:
#
#
#       telemetry
#
#       audit
#
#       event store
#
#
# rather than the current evaluator.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — ENDPOINT SOURCE FAILURE
# ============================================================================
#
# If one endpoint source fails:
#
#
#       Kubernetes unavailable
#
#
# another source might still report:
#
#
#       service registry says endpoint exists.
#
#
# Future architecture may need degraded evidence semantics.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# MULTIPLE SOURCES MAY IMPROVE RESILIENCE
# ============================================================================
#
# But they also create:
#
#
#       reconciliation
#
#       trust
#
#       freshness
#
# complexities.
#
#
# Do not add redundant sources without understanding these costs.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — AUTHORITATIVE SOURCE
# ============================================================================
#
# Perhaps Kubernetes is authoritative for Kubernetes backend state.
#
# Perhaps an internal platform registry is authoritative for service identity.
#
# Perhaps cloud networking is authoritative for private endpoint state.
#
#
# Different domains may have different authoritative sources.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# ONE UNIVERSAL SOURCE OF TRUTH MAY NOT EXIST
# ============================================================================
#
# Distributed systems often have:
#
#
#       several authoritative sources for different facts.
#
#
# Preserve fact ownership rather than forcing everything into one registry.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — CROSS-CLOUD IDENTITY
# ============================================================================
#
# A service may migrate or replicate across:
#
#
#       AWS
#
#       Azure
#
#       GCP
#
#
# Infrastructure identifiers will differ.
#
#
# Agent 11 service identity should remain provider-neutral.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — PROVIDER SDK ADAPTERS
# ============================================================================
#
# Endpoint evidence from:
#
#
#       AWS
#
#       Azure
#
#       GCP
#
#
# may eventually require provider adapters.
#
#
# Provider SDKs should remain at infrastructure boundaries.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# PROVIDER SDK != DOMAIN
# ============================================================================
#
# Preserve.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — INFRASTRUCTURE AS CODE
# ============================================================================
#
# Terraform may know intended endpoint topology.
#
#
# Kubernetes may know observed topology.
#
#
# Those are different:
#
#
#       DESIRED STATE
#
#           !=
#
#       OBSERVED STATE
#
#
# ============================================================================
# TERRAFORM SAYS EXISTS != ENDPOINT CURRENTLY EXISTS
# ============================================================================
#
# Terraform state can be stale.
#
#
# It should not automatically substitute for runtime observation.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# DESIRED STATE != CURRENT STATE
# ============================================================================
#
# Preserve.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — GITOPS
# ============================================================================
#
# GitOps may describe desired deployment/service state.
#
#
# Again:
#
#
#       Git says should exist
#
#           !=
#
#       endpoint currently exists
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — CONTROLLER STATUS
# ============================================================================
#
# Kubernetes CRD status fields may offer useful observed state.
#
#
# Treat them as evidence produced by a controller.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# CONTROLLER STATUS != ABSOLUTE TRUTH
# ============================================================================
#
# The controller itself may be stale or unhealthy.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — ERROR TAXONOMY
# ============================================================================
#
# If endpoint evidence becomes richer, errors may need categories such as:
#
#
#       mapping missing
#
#       source unavailable
#
#       access denied
#
#       no endpoint observed
#
#       stale observation
#
#
# But:
#
#
#       DO NOT BUILD THE TAXONOMY
#       UNTIL OPERATIONS NEED IT.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# TELEMETRY BEFORE TAXONOMY
# ============================================================================
#
# Observe real failure patterns first.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — SECURITY EVENT
# ============================================================================
#
# If Kubernetes RBAC denies endpoint discovery:
#
#
# that may mean:
#
#
#       misconfiguration
#
#       security change
#
#       credential expiration
#
#
# Do not classify:
#
#
#       access denied
#
# as:
#
#       no endpoint exists.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# ACCESS DENIED != ABSENT
# ============================================================================
#
# Preserve.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — TIME OF CHECK / TIME OF USE
# ============================================================================
#
# Endpoint may be observed:
#
#
#       ready
#
#
# and disappear milliseconds before invocation.
#
#
# No distributed system can completely eliminate this race.
#
#
# Future routing/recovery should understand:
#
#
#       evaluation reduces uncertainty
#
#       it does not freeze infrastructure.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# CHECKED != GUARANTEED FOREVER
# ============================================================================
#
# Preserve.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — REQUEST-SCOPED SNAPSHOT
# ============================================================================
#
# Some workflows may eventually preserve:
#
#
#       routing evidence snapshot
#
#
# used to explain why a decision was made.
#
#
# That does not mean the snapshot remains current throughout execution.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# DECISION EVIDENCE != CURRENT INFRASTRUCTURE
# ============================================================================
#
# Preserve.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — FRESH RE-EVALUATION AFTER FAILURE
# ============================================================================
#
# This connects directly to fallback.py.
#
#
# If an endpoint fails after initial routing:
#
#
#       fresh endpoint evidence
#
#       fresh health evidence
#
#       fresh path evidence
#
#
# should participate in the new routing cycle.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# HISTORICAL ENDPOINT EVIDENCE != CURRENT ENDPOINT EVIDENCE
# ============================================================================
#
# Same temporal rule as routing candidates.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — ENDPOINT OBSERVATION COST
# ============================================================================
#
# Querying many clusters on every fallback attempt may be expensive/slow.
#
#
# This may eventually justify caches or regional observers.
#
#
# Optimize architecture when the operational cost is known.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — BATCH OBSERVATION
# ============================================================================
#
# A network orchestrator might eventually request endpoint evidence for many
# services at once.
#
#
# That may justify a batch source API later.
#
#
# Do not complicate the current Protocol now.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# ONE-AT-A-TIME API != PERMANENT PERFORMANCE MODEL
# ============================================================================
#
# Preserve.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — WATCHING MANY CLUSTERS
# ============================================================================
#
# A central process maintaining watches to 100 clusters may be operationally
# fragile.
#
#
# Possible future alternatives:
#
#
#       regional observers
#
#       event forwarding
#
#       fleet control plane
#
#       distributed cache
#
#
# But:
#
#
#       infrastructure topology
#
#
# should not force changes to endpoint semantics.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — SECURITY OF WATCH EVENTS
# ============================================================================
#
# If observations arrive remotely:
#
#
#       authenticate source
#
#       protect transport
#
#       preserve provenance
#
#
# becomes important.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — NAMESPACES AT SCALE
# ============================================================================
#
# Hundreds of namespaces may exist.
#
#
# Avoid endpoint adapters scanning:
#
#
#       every namespace
#
#
# for:
#
#       every request
#
#
# if deterministic mapping exists.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# DISCOVERY STRATEGY != ENDPOINT SEMANTICS
# ============================================================================
#
# Preserve.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — K8S API RATE LIMITS
# ============================================================================
#
# Endpoint discovery may itself be throttled.
#
#
# This should appear as:
#
#
#       observation problem
#
#
# not:
#
#       endpoint absence.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — PERMISSION BOUNDARIES BY NAMESPACE
# ============================================================================
#
# Least privilege may give each observer access only to specific namespaces.
#
#
# That may require:
#
#       several clients
#
#       several ServiceAccounts
#
#       several adapter instances
#
#
# IoC should make that composition possible.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# ONE CLIENT != ONE ORGANIZATION
# ============================================================================
#
# Preserve.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — MULTI-TENANT CLUSTERS
# ============================================================================
#
# One Kubernetes cluster may host services from:
#
#
#       several teams
#
#       several business units
#
#       several tenants
#
#
# Endpoint discovery authority should remain scoped.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# CLUSTER ACCESS != ACCESS TO EVERY SERVICE
# ============================================================================
#
# Preserve.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — PLATFORM TEAM BOUNDARY
# ============================================================================
#
# Platform engineering may own:
#
#
#       Kubernetes
#
#       Gateway API
#
#       service mesh
#
#
# while AI/security teams own:
#
#
#       model registry
#
#       AI policy
#
#       routing semantics
#
#
# Agent 11 should respect those ownership boundaries.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# ORGANIZATIONAL OWNERSHIP MAY INFORM COMPONENT OWNERSHIP
# ============================================================================
#
# Useful future design signal.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — ENDPOINT CONFIGURATION OWNERSHIP
# ============================================================================
#
# Who maps:
#
#
#       AIService.service_id
#
#           ->
#
#       Kubernetes namespace/service?
#
#
# Possible owners:
#
#
#       platform configuration
#
#       model registry
#
#       deployment registry
#
#
# Decide explicitly before implementation.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# MAPPING OWNERSHIP MATTERS
# ============================================================================
#
# Because changing the mapping changes where Agent 11 may eventually send
# requests.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — CONFIGURATION VALIDATION
# ============================================================================
#
# If mappings become configuration:
#
#
#       validate structure
#
#       validate uniqueness
#
#       validate references
#
#
# But:
#
#
#       valid mapping syntax
#
#           !=
#
#       trusted mapping authority.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — DUPLICATE MAPPINGS
# ============================================================================
#
# What if one service_id maps to:
#
#
#       cluster A / service X
#
#       cluster B / service Y
#
#
# Is that:
#
#
#       redundancy
#
#       configuration error
#
#       multi-deployment?
#
#
# Domain semantics must answer.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# DO NOT LET dict[str, str] BECOME AN ACCIDENTAL DEPLOYMENT MODEL
# ============================================================================
#
# If mappings become complicated, introduce a coherent contract rather than
# stuffing meaning into nested dictionaries.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — ENDPOINT ORDERING
# ============================================================================
#
# Endpoint evidence should not silently imply:
#
#
#       preferred endpoint.
#
#
# Preference belongs to routing/path/deployment selection.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# ENDPOINT EXISTS != ENDPOINT PREFERRED
# ============================================================================
#
# Preserve.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — LOCALITY PREFERENCE
# ============================================================================
#
# Closer endpoint may be:
#
#
#       faster
#
#       cheaper
#
#
# but:
#
#       not policy-permitted
#
#
# or:
#
#       not resilient.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# CLOSER != PERMITTED
# ============================================================================
#
# Preserve.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — ROUTE OPTIMIZATION
# ============================================================================
#
# Endpoint data may eventually contribute to:
#
#
#       latency optimization
#
#       cost optimization
#
#       topology optimization
#
#
# But optimization occurs only after hard constraints.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# ENDPOINT METADATA MUST NOT BECOME POLICY SCORE
# ============================================================================
#
# Preserve.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — MODEL SECURITY ASSURANCE
# ============================================================================
#
# A network endpoint may be secure while:
#
#
#       model version unapproved
#
#
# Therefore:
#
#
#       SECURE ENDPOINT
#           !=
#       APPROVED MODEL
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — SERVICE SECURITY ASSURANCE
# ============================================================================
#
# Similarly:
#
#
#       model approved
#
#
# does not necessarily mean:
#
#
#       service deployment approved.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# MODEL ASSURANCE != ENDPOINT ASSURANCE
# ============================================================================
#
# Preserve.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — ENDPOINT IDENTITY CERTIFICATES
# ============================================================================
#
# mTLS certificates may eventually provide endpoint/workload identity.
#
#
# Certificate subject/SAN values may be infrastructure evidence.
#
#
# Do not expose raw certificates through Agent 11 domain layers unless needed.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# CERTIFICATE VALID != ROUTE AUTHORIZED
# ============================================================================
#
# Preserve.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — SPIFFE / SPIRE
# ============================================================================
#
# Workload identity systems may eventually provide:
#
#
#       cryptographic service identity
#
#
# useful for endpoint trust.
#
#
# Again:
#
#
#       IDENTITY VERIFIED
#           !=
#       DATA ROUTE AUTHORIZED
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — SERVICE ACCOUNT IDENTITY
# ============================================================================
#
# Kubernetes ServiceAccount may identify the workload making observations.
#
#
# It does not necessarily identify the model/backend serving inference.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# CALLER IDENTITY != DESTINATION IDENTITY
# ============================================================================
#
# Preserve.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — ENDPOINT SECURITY GROUPS / FIREWALLS
# ============================================================================
#
# These affect path reachability.
#
#
# Keep them out of endpoint existence.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — ROUTE TABLES
# ============================================================================
#
# Same.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — SUBNETS
# ============================================================================
#
# Endpoint may reside in subnet.
#
#
# Subnet location may inform path/topology.
#
#
# Endpoint identity should not become the entire network topology model.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# ENDPOINT != NETWORK TOPOLOGY
# ============================================================================
#
# Preserve.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — NAT
# ============================================================================
#
# NAT may affect observable source/destination addressing.
#
#
# This belongs in path/network architecture if required.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — PROXIES
# ============================================================================
#
# Agent 11 may eventually invoke an endpoint through:
#
#
#       corporate proxy
#
#       egress gateway
#
#       service mesh sidecar
#
#
# Actual packet destination may differ from logical service destination.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# LOGICAL DESTINATION != EVERY NETWORK HOP
# ============================================================================
#
# Endpoint model should not become traceroute.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — EGRESS GATEWAYS
# ============================================================================
#
# Egress gateways may enforce:
#
#
#       network controls
#
#       logging
#
#       TLS inspection
#
#
# Those are path/security infrastructure.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — DATA LOSS PREVENTION AT NETWORK EDGE
# ============================================================================
#
# DLP may inspect outbound traffic.
#
#
# That may provide another security control.
#
#
# It does not replace Agent 11 policy.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# DEFENSE IN DEPTH != DUPLICATE DOMAIN OWNERSHIP
# ============================================================================
#
# Multiple controls may enforce related requirements.
#
#
# They should still preserve clear responsibilities.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — INCIDENT QUARANTINE
# ============================================================================
#
# Security may mark an endpoint/deployment:
#
#
#       quarantined
#
#
# This probably belongs to service/deployment/policy state rather than:
#
#
#       endpoint absent.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# QUARANTINED ENDPOINT MAY STILL BE DISCOVERABLE
# ============================================================================
#
# Discovery should remain honest.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — ENDPOINT RETIREMENT
# ============================================================================
#
# Endpoint may remain discoverable briefly after logical service retirement.
#
#
# Another reason:
#
#
#       endpoint exists
#
#           !=
#       service active.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — EVENTUAL DELETE
# ============================================================================
#
# Kubernetes deletion is asynchronous.
#
#
# terminating objects may remain visible.
#
#
# Adapter semantics may eventually need to account for this.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — KUBERNETES finalizers
# ============================================================================
#
# Another infrastructure detail that should not leak into Agent 11 unless it
# affects required evidence.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# FUTURE QUESTION — NETWORK PARTITION
# ============================================================================
